  "fields": {
    "title": "Hypertext",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Hypertext, by enabling the creation of a functional, entirely new text structure in digital space, has potentially changed the linearity of text forever. The term was coined by Theodor H. Nelson in the 1960s and refers to a form of electronic text that enables non-sequential writing and reading.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "6pNnsTO58A3VwCf8hYqCvF",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[1]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " In general, hypertext is defined as a unit of text, images and data linked via hyperlinks to form an overall text. This means that it has a dynamic, non-linear structure that breaks up the linear sequentiality of classic print publications. Texts therefore have no predefined reading order and contain cross-references called hyperlinks that lead to other texts or content of any form.  Through links, the interconnected content inevitably enters into a relationship with each other and opens up space for discourse.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "65L7k55DfO8e0NirwFV7nr",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[2]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " The hypertext thus has no unity like conventional texts and can be described more as a text collection or hypertextual text structure. This allows not only a variety of reading possibilities, but also possibilities of interpretation.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "ukeUgoOXuoMsDgTOs5eTz",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[3]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " Classically, hypertext is written in mark-up languages such as HTML, which are not primarily visible to the reader, but can be viewed in the browser using developer tools. The term hypertext is also often used as a synonym for hypermedia, which extends the factor of non-linearity by that of multimedia.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "1uEWgvtF1KSfHXkkkoRH4i",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[4]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " Hypermedia extends the concept of ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "text",
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " in hypertext by including visual information, sound, animation and other forms of data. Hypertext thus refers to an information medium that combines verbal and non-verbal information.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "b4uAJQvfzmOMAW8EG5jav",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[5]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " The postmodern philosophical concept of the ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "3RJQsZ5m8f42WS3r189dDe",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "rhizome",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": ", which derives from the root system of the plant rhizome, is often used to describe the structure of hypertext.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Background",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The hypertext, and in particular its characteristic of the non-linearity of text, was euphorically welcomed by many representatives of postmodern and poststructuralist currents. The new technology was seen as a confirmation of the postmodern literary theory popular in the 1980s, which can be traced back to the ideas of Barthes, Foucault, Lyotard, Derrida, Deleuze and Guattari.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2azhuQeSXtEFB3CRFQSmz5",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[6]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " In ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "S/Z",
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": ", Roland Barthes described in 1973 an ideal textuality that corresponds pretty much to what is now called hypertext. This textuality consists of blocks of words (or images) that are electronically connected by a multitude of paths, chains or trails in an open, eternally unfinished textuality.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "6pNnsTO58A3VwCf8hYqCvF",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[7]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "blockquote",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "paragraph",
              "data": {},
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "»In this ideal text, the networks are many and interact, without any one of them being able to surpass the rest; this text is a galaxy of signifiers, not a structure of signifieds; it has no beginning; it is reversible; we gain access to it by several entrances, none of which can be authoritatively declared to be the main one; the codes it mobilizes extend as far as the eye can reach, they are indeterminable (...) ; the systems of meaning can take over this absolutely plural text, but their number is never closed, based as it is on the infinity of language.« ",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
                  "data": {
                    "target": {
                      "sys": {
                        "id": "3hB2E2wGGEDuEuwZEagvcl",
                        "type": "Link",
                        "linkType": "Entry"
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "nodeType": "text",
                      "value": "[8]",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "superscript"
                        }
                      ],
                      "data": {}
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Like Barthes, Michel Foucault also understands the text as a network of connections. But he considers this network to be possible not only in the digital space, but also in the form of the printed book. In ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The Archaeology of Knowledge",
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " he points out that the boundaries of a book are never clearly drawn because it is integrated into a system of references to other books, other texts and other sentences and thus functions as a node in a network of references. It is striking that structuralists and post-structuralists, such as Barthes and Foucault, describe the text, the world of letters and the power and status relations associated with it in terms that are also used in the field of computer hypertext.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "6pNnsTO58A3VwCf8hYqCvF",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[7]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
